WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:09.680 --> 00:00:14.180
A mere 542 million years ago, the Earth's landscape was a barren wilderness.

00:00:14.900 --> 00:00:19.200
4 billion years after the planet's creation, no animals lived on the land.

00:00:19.560 --> 00:00:23.090
The most developed organisms were simple animals living in the oceans.

00:00:24.509 --> 00:00:26.719
Something extraordinary was happening in the seas.

00:00:27.869 --> 00:00:31.848
Over a period of 20 million years, at the beginning of a period called the Cambrian, an

00:00:32.430 --> 00:00:34.880
explosion of new animals developed below the water's surface.

00:00:35.730 --> 00:00:40.399
These later gave rise to most of the diversity of animal life that now exists on Earth.

00:00:41.730 --> 00:00:45.829
But the cause [of] this sudden burst of life,  which geologists call the cambrian explosion,

00:00:46.500 --> 00:00:48.500
remains a mystery.

00:00:48.629 --> 00:00:55.518
The Cambrian explosion was really Biology's big bang. Things went from moving very slowly along the sea floor without any

00:00:55.829 --> 00:01:01.999
predation to suddenly all the different type of ecologies that we find today. Your life literally exploded.

00:01:03.570 --> 00:01:08.809
What triggered the cambrian explosion? We don't really know. There are many different possibilities, different hypotheses

00:01:08.810 --> 00:01:12.470
and we've got to remember [that] cause and effect are often difficult to disentangle.

00:01:13.140 --> 00:01:15.140
So what happened exactly?

00:01:15.600 --> 00:01:20.419
To try to understand it, you need to look a little bit farther back in the history of the Earth to the

00:01:20.610 --> 00:01:26.540
Ediacaran period which preceded the Cambrian. It was during this time that the first large animals evolved,

00:01:26.540 --> 00:01:28.540
but they were shell-less.

00:01:28.710 --> 00:01:32.930
Before the Cambrian, we go into what's called the Ediacaran times.

00:01:32.930 --> 00:01:37.700
And this is an extraordinary interval where we have the seafloor

00:01:38.790 --> 00:01:44.690
populated by basically weird creatures.  And quite frankly, we're not quite sure whether they're animals or something else entirely.

00:01:45.660 --> 00:01:50.749
These are organisms which seem to have mostly survived on absorbing

00:01:51.390 --> 00:01:56.450
nutrients from the seawater itself. They may not have had any gut.  Some of them may be able to move.

00:01:56.730 --> 00:02:00.619
These creatures are principally soft bodied, that is without a skeleton,

00:02:00.620 --> 00:02:04.910
and then they seem to more or less disappear as the Cambrian explosion kicks off.

00:02:06.210 --> 00:02:13.729
After 45 million years of slow evolution those weird creatures suddenly disappeared to be replaced by those of the Cambrian.

00:02:14.760 --> 00:02:18.590
The first Echinoderms appeared 540 million years ago.

00:02:19.350 --> 00:02:21.619
Starfish and sea urchins are their descendants.

00:02:22.350 --> 00:02:25.129
Brachiopods followed after some ten million years.

00:02:25.620 --> 00:02:33.110
The trilobite, the cambrian's most iconic fossil, joined the explosion towards its end. It showed up a few million years later.

00:02:34.380 --> 00:02:38.509
There are a number of theories about what happened that enabled these creatures to appear.

00:02:44.880 --> 00:02:46.650
There's a theory, a

00:02:46.650 --> 00:02:52.880
hypothesis, suggesting that one of the factors that holding back the evolution of animal is

00:02:53.310 --> 00:02:55.640
the availability of oxygen.

00:02:57.150 --> 00:02:59.360
Photosynthesizers, things like cyanobacteria,

00:03:00.720 --> 00:03:03.800
algae, plants, make oxygen and

00:03:04.470 --> 00:03:11.269
organic carbon. And once the organic carbon is buried in the sediment, the oxygen is released in the

00:03:11.580 --> 00:03:13.470
ocean and the atmosphere

00:03:13.470 --> 00:03:15.470
for animals to use.

00:03:16.560 --> 00:03:18.420
Prior to the Cambrian explosion

00:03:18.420 --> 00:03:22.070
oxygen levels may have been too low for large active animals to survive,

00:03:22.680 --> 00:03:27.110
but once those levels increased, larger animals were able to survive and proliferate.

00:03:28.530 --> 00:03:33.860
Then animals burst and diversified in many many different forms.

00:03:35.970 --> 00:03:43.160
Evidence to support this theory of higher oxygen levels is scarce, but there may be a link to a phenomenon called snowball Earth.

00:03:43.829 --> 00:03:51.349
This was a long period of recurrent ice ages.  When the glaciers melted huge amount of rock were exposed and eroded

00:03:51.630 --> 00:03:58.609
pouring nutrients into the water that encouraged the growth of photosynthetic algae, which would have caused oxygen levels to rise.

00:03:59.830 --> 00:04:04.830
But the last of the giant ice ages ended around 90 million years before the cambrian.

00:04:05.530 --> 00:04:12.989
Things like oxygen levels which are fairly clearly increasing during this interval close to the cambrian explosion must have been important

00:04:13.239 --> 00:04:16.948
but oxygen was accumulating already much, much before then.

00:04:18.190 --> 00:04:25.320
Another theory for why the cambrian explosion occurred is that minerals became increasingly available in the oceans due to post glacial erosion.

00:04:26.020 --> 00:04:28.859
These could have been used for the construction of shells, but

00:04:29.590 --> 00:04:31.590
cambrian shells were made from a range of materials.

00:04:32.139 --> 00:04:37.978
This suggests that they were the result of parallel evolution in different animal lines not a single innovation.

00:04:41.560 --> 00:04:45.419
That means something encouraged them to develop on more than one occasion and

00:04:45.729 --> 00:04:50.669
shells are costly to make. It's unlikely mere abundance of minerals would have been enough.

00:04:51.910 --> 00:04:55.529
Could a transformative innovation in the animals themselves be the explanation?

00:04:56.320 --> 00:05:02.669
One such innovation could be bilateral symmetry which concentrates nerves and sense organs at an animals front and

00:05:03.039 --> 00:05:06.329
encourages the development of an efficient gut with a mouth and an anus.

00:05:10.540 --> 00:05:14.580
We are inventing nervous systems or inventing eyes and other sensory systems.

00:05:14.580 --> 00:05:19.800
We're learning how to move fast, how to swim effectively. So you have a whole cascade of feedbacks.

00:05:19.810 --> 00:05:27.060
And it's really trying to pinpoint if you like the trigger versus all the other consequences which flowed from that initial point.

00:05:28.670 --> 00:05:30.670
The cambrian explosion

00:05:30.980 --> 00:05:35.020
happens after a series of events in my opinion.

00:05:35.540 --> 00:05:39.189
First you need to have genetic building blocks

00:05:39.380 --> 00:05:43.390
to make an animal.  You also need oxygen to

00:05:43.850 --> 00:05:48.640
support animals, to support animal metabolism. But that alone is

00:05:49.580 --> 00:05:52.419
not sufficient either. You also need a mechanism

00:05:52.940 --> 00:05:59.320
to drive the system into a runaway situation. You need to make the system work

00:05:59.690 --> 00:06:04.809
very fast.  And one of the mechanism to drive the system to a runaway

00:06:05.420 --> 00:06:07.040
situation is

00:06:07.040 --> 00:06:09.040
ecological feedback.

00:06:10.400 --> 00:06:14.409
Ecological feedback, in this sense, refers to the interaction between predator and prey.

00:06:14.960 --> 00:06:20.109
During the Cambrian, predators evolved new mechanisms such as vision to track down their lunch. A

00:06:20.690 --> 00:06:26.080
good part of what's happening in the Cambrian explosion must be the motor of ecological change. And

00:06:26.750 --> 00:06:28.750
one of the really big

00:06:28.760 --> 00:06:30.130
important gear changes,

00:06:30.130 --> 00:06:35.710
so to speak, to pursue that analogy, is in the way in which predators and prey

00:06:35.990 --> 00:06:41.949
interact. Part of the explanation for the widespread development of skeletons must be the rise of predators.

00:06:41.950 --> 00:06:48.369
And we see other evidence in terms of mouth parts, even gut content sometimes.  You can see what one animal swallowed.

00:06:48.560 --> 00:06:55.510
And all these things suggests that we are moving into an ecology which is radically different from anything ever before.  An

00:06:56.510 --> 00:07:03.280
integral part of this is the evolution of nervous systems.  And nervous systems of course include ultimately brains and

00:07:03.800 --> 00:07:10.449
ultimately of course when animals come onto land. At some times even in the oceans big brains which start thinking, "oh".

00:07:12.610 --> 00:07:18.750
Some believe the emergence of vision during the cambrian period caused the rate of ecological change to accelerate.  I

00:07:19.750 --> 00:07:24.239
believe that vision must have played a significant role. I think the fact that that event

00:07:25.330 --> 00:07:27.419
coincides with the start of the cambrian explosion

00:07:28.780 --> 00:07:33.719
possibly could indicate that vision was actually the only thing that was required to trigger the cambrian explosion.

00:07:35.229 --> 00:07:39.628
Light today is the most powerful stimulus of all and vision is the most powerful sense.

00:07:40.419 --> 00:07:44.818
When that's unleashed on the world for the first time it's going to create chaos.

00:07:45.759 --> 00:07:47.968
Now the capability to sense light

00:07:48.849 --> 00:07:54.509
has been there for a long time.  Even some bacteria have a way to sense light.

00:07:55.240 --> 00:08:01.259
Many microorganisms have a way to sense light. But the very complex eyes,

00:08:01.960 --> 00:08:03.960
they can very precisely

00:08:04.060 --> 00:08:10.529
position the prey, they can view three dimensions and allow the predators to very effectively catch the prey.

00:08:10.659 --> 00:08:15.749
That's a very important part of the ecological feedback that allows the cambrian

00:08:16.060 --> 00:08:18.750
explosion to happen in a very rapid rate.

00:08:20.529 --> 00:08:24.869
Now we have this pinpoint accuracy, a predator could spot a prey and

00:08:24.969 --> 00:08:25.469
also

00:08:25.469 --> 00:08:30.838
because it's got its fast swimming parts and hard part itself, it could get to that animal instantly and tear it apart.

00:08:31.120 --> 00:08:36.060
So that means now there's huge selection pressures on all those soft bodies animals to evolve

00:08:36.399 --> 00:08:40.289
defenses against this which means really hard parts. It means

00:08:40.810 --> 00:08:45.119
shields and spines and also hard swimming parts to swim away itself.

00:08:45.760 --> 00:08:47.620
But not everyone agrees.

00:08:47.620 --> 00:08:53.609
Now there is absolutely no doubt that eyes did evolve as part of the cambrian explosion.  My reservation with it

00:08:53.610 --> 00:08:59.310
is that eyes come in all sorts of shapes and forms.  And primitive eyes are really little more than what we call eye spots.

00:08:59.310 --> 00:09:03.539
They're sensitive really to sort of shades of grey and white and no more than that.

00:09:03.540 --> 00:09:08.670
And that's quite sufficient for the great majority of animals. They don't actually make an image on their eye.  Whereas

00:09:08.670 --> 00:09:13.019
I think one of the dangers, if I may say so, was seeing evolution through our eyes.

00:09:14.110 --> 00:09:18.490
We have fantastically well adapted eyes which are the product of millions of years of evolution

00:09:18.490 --> 00:09:23.529
And there's little doubt that those really sophisticated eyes only evolved a bit later.

00:09:26.839 --> 00:09:28.869
There is one other possibility.

00:09:29.630 --> 00:09:35.200
The disappearance of the Ediacaran creatures may be the cause, not the consequence of the cambrian explosion.

00:09:35.990 --> 00:09:39.099
The explosion resembles the recovery from a mass extinction.

00:09:41.750 --> 00:09:49.059
Signs of a catastrophe 542 million years ago could easily have vanished by now, but that doesn't mean one didn't happen.

00:09:51.140 --> 00:09:57.849
Perhaps the bilateral ancestors of today's animals were simply hiding in the shadows of the ediacaran world until opportunity knocked.

00:09:59.660 --> 00:10:05.589
Whatever actually did happen though, they took that opportunity with momentous consequences for the future.

00:10:06.589 --> 00:10:09.159
Humans are direct descendants of the cambrian explosion.

00:10:09.670 --> 00:10:11.329
we have primitive fish

00:10:11.329 --> 00:10:16.299
From deposits such as a burgess shale in Canada which we can really say are our ancestors.

00:10:17.060 --> 00:10:24.190
From the human point of view, the true significance of the cambrian explosion is that Homo Sapiens wouldn't be here if it hadn't happened.

